# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.pyo
.venv/

# SQLite
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# Vault identity + migration/rekey artifacts - never commit any of these.
# salt.bin/verifier.bin/kdf.json are the vault's key-derivation identity;
# *.plain.bak-* is the plaintext DB backup the one-time migration leaves;
# *.bak/*.new are crash-safe rekey temporaries.
#
# The trailing * on the three identity names is the whole point and was missing
# until 20 Aug 2026. crypto.py never writes a bare `.bak`: initialize(),
# change_passphrase() and its shelve path all write
# `<name>.bak-<unix ts>`, which `*.bak` does NOT match - the pattern wants a
# name ENDING in `.bak`. The old comment named "verifier.bin.bak" as if that
# were the shape on disk; it never was. In a dev checkout DATA_DIR is
# `backend/`, so a reset or a passphrase change dropped
# `backend/salt.bin.bak-<ts>` and `backend/verifier.bin.bak-<ts>` straight into
# the work tree as untracked files that `git add -A` would have staged, into a
# PUBLIC repo. salt + verifier together are an offline, unlimited-rate oracle
# against the passphrase - which is exactly why the bare names are ignored.
# The history was scanned when this was found: neither has ever been committed.
# kdf.json had the same hole for the live file (only kdf.json.new was covered
# by *.new); routers/vault.py's _reset_identity_files and tests/fs_guard.py
# treat the four as one
# unit, so the ignore rules now do too.
salt.bin*
verifier.bin*
kdf.json*
# The salt mirror. Same material as salt.bin and therefore the same rule, and
# the trailing star for the same reason the others carry one: crypto.py
# writes <name>.new and <name>.bak-<unix ts> beside it, and a pattern without
# the star matches none of them.
vault.recovery*
*.plain.bak-*
*.bak
*.new
# Crash-recovery copies, named during the KÖK 18 work: app.db.enc-tmp is a
# COMPLETE encrypted copy of the vault left by an interrupted migration,
# and app.db.empty-stub-bak is the 0-byte live file adoption moves aside.
# The trailing * covers app.db.enc-tmp.orphan.bak-<ts>: migration moves a copy
# it finds in its way ASIDE rather than deleting it, and a complete vault that
# git could pick up is the last thing this list may miss.
*.enc-tmp*
*.empty-stub-bak

# Environment
.env
.env.*
# ...except the template. `.env.*` was swallowing .env.example, so the one file
# whose whole job is to show a new clone what to create could never reach the
# clone. It holds a commented-out default URL and nothing else.
!.env.example

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Editor
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# Node / Build artifacts
node_modules/
dist/
frontend/dist/
.next/
.vite/
*.local

# PyInstaller work dir + build logs
build/
build_log*.txt
.pytest_cache/

# The constraint snapshot used to regenerate requirements.lock.txt. It is a
# freeze of one machine's venv, not a fact about the project, and uv writes
# whatever path it is given into 45 provenance comments in the lockfile - so it
# has to live here, beside requirements.txt, and never be committed.
backend/current.txt

# Backend debug tools (dumps DB content - never commit)
backend/_show_db.py

# Secrets - must never be committed
# Anchored and extension-bound on purpose: a bare `keyring*` matches at ANY
# depth, so it swallowed backend/keyring_service.py - a module imported at
# module scope by routers/settings.py and legacy_migration.py. The published
# tree therefore raised ModuleNotFoundError before the app could serve
# anything, and `git add` refused the file with no hint that an ignore rule
# was the cause.
/keyring.json
/keyring.db
keyring*.json
keyring*.db
*.key
*.pem

# Avatars (user-uploaded binary data)
backend/avatars/

# Image attachments (user-uploaded binary data)
backend/uploads/

# Desktop launcher's WebView2 profile (dev runs of run_app.py put it here)
backend/webview/

# ── Everything the running app writes about ITS USER ────────────────────
# In dev, DATA_DIR resolves to backend/, so every path config.py derives
# from it lands inside this repository. None of it is source, and all of it
# is either the user's own words, their voice, their pictures or their keys.
#
# Hardcoded prompts are NOT this: voice_tags.py's delivery-tag block and the
# character/persona system blocks are code, and they stay tracked.
backend/app.db*
backend/logs/
*.log
# The port the server last bound to. Not a secret - it is one integer - but it
# is written by run_app into DATA_DIR like everything above, and the rule for
# this block is "whatever the running app writes", not "whatever looks
# dangerous". It was the one runtime artefact sitting outside the net.
backend/port

# Voice / TTS data dir (dev runs resolve DATA_DIR to backend/). Holds the
# user's downloaded model weights, their reference voice recordings and the
# transcripts of those recordings - multi-GB binaries AND personal audio.
# None of it may ever reach the repo: the app ships the TTS infrastructure
# only, never a model.
backend/voice/

# Assistant / editor tooling state. .serena/memories holds notes written
# ABOUT this project - quoted code, decisions, sometimes chat content - and
# its cache is machine-local. Neither is source.
.serena/
.claude/

# Internal planning / implementation docs.
#
# NOT the frontend contract: docs/frontend_contract.md is the contract of
# record between the two halves of the app, and two test files read it as
# their source of truth (test_tts_contract.py, test_release_sync.py). With
# the whole directory ignored those tests skip on a fresh clone, so the
# document everything else is checked against was itself unpublished.
# docs/* not docs/ - git cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is
# excluded, so the negations below are dead against a bare `docs/`.
docs/*
!docs/frontend_contract.md
!docs/AUDIT_2026_07_CLOSURE.md

# Throwaway data directories used when the packaged exe is self-tested
# (ELYSIUM_DATA_DIR pointed somewhere disposable). An interrupted run leaves
# a COMPLETE vault behind - encrypted, but still the user's conversation -
# and the rule for this repository is that nothing the running app writes is
# ever a candidate for a commit. `*.db` catches the database itself; this
# catches the directory, its logs and its voice folder with it.
/.st/
/.selftest_tmp/
